"""Registry data for the Anaplan scraper toolchain.

PURE DATA. No imports, no environment lookups, no logic - all of that lives
in the tracked tools/registry.py. This file is gitignored because it holds
tenant GUIDs and customer folder names.

Why literals and not environment lookups: models.py used to resolve every
GUID from the environment at import time, so any consumer that imported it
before `.env` had been loaded silently got None for every id - which showed
up live as a request to .../models/None/views/... The values are literals
now, so the failure mode is structurally impossible.

KEEP IT THAT WAY. tools/test_registry.py asserts that this file's source
contains neither of the two function names involved in that incident - not
even inside a comment or this docstring - because a plain substring tripwire
is the only version of that check that cannot itself be fooled. If you need
to refer to those mechanisms, describe them rather than naming them, as this
docstring does.

SHAPE

    CUSTOMERS = {
        "<customer_key>": {
            "name":        display name,
            "shard":       bare Anaplan app shard token, e.g. "eu2a", "eu3"
            "folder":      folder under customers/ holding this customer's vault
            "customer_id": tenant GUID
            "models": {
                "<model_key>": {
                    "name":            display name,
                    "raw_dir":         folder under
                                       customers/<folder>/raw/models/
                    "workspace_id":    workspace GUID
                    "model_id":        model GUID
                    "engine":          "Classic" | "Polaris"   (optional,
                                       defaults to "unknown")
                    "workspace_label": "DEV" | "PRODUCTION"    (optional,
                                       defaults to "PRODUCTION")
                },
            },
        },
    }

Customer fields are inherited by every model under that customer, so the
shard and tenant GUID are declared ONCE. A model may override an inherited
field (a second workspace on another shard is legal); the model's own value
wins.

Keys become halves of a "customer:model" composite, so they may contain only
letters, digits, '_', '.' and '-' - no ':' and no whitespace. Address a model
as "customera:modela", or by its bare model key when no other customer uses
that key.

`raw_dir` is a separate key from `name` because shortcut keys are
abbreviations a modeller types and drift from folder names as soon as a model
is renamed: a shortcut called "modela" can point at a folder called
"ModelA 2.0". No fuzzy match can bridge them.

Find a new customer's GUIDs without hand-editing anything:

    python tools/scrape_model_data.py --list-models --shard eu3 > models.json

or, once the customer exists here, get a paste-ready snippet:

    python tools/scrape_model_data.py --list-models --customer customera --emit-config
"""

CUSTOMERS = {
    # "customera": {
    #     "name": "CustomerA",
    #     "shard": "eu2a",
    #     "folder": "CustomerA",
    #     "customer_id": "00000000000000000000000000000000",
    #     "models": {
    #         "modela": {
    #             "name": "ModelA",
    #             "raw_dir": "ModelA 2.0",
    #             "workspace_id": "00000000000000000000000000000000",
    #             "model_id": "00000000000000000000000000000000",
    #             "engine": "Polaris",
    #             "workspace_label": "DEV",
    #         },
    #     },
    # },
}
